[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.9]
\fill[blue!50] (0,0) -- (90:1.2) arc[start angle=90, end angle=180, radius=1.2] -- cycle;
\draw (0,0) circle (1.2);
\foreach \a in {0,90,180,270} {\draw (0,0)--(\a:1.2);}
\node at (0,-1.7) {Spinner A};
\fill[blue!50] (3.6,0) -- ($(3.6,0)+(90:1.2)$) arc[start angle=90, end angle=135, radius=1.2] -- cycle;
\draw (3.6,0) circle (1.2);
\foreach \a in {0,45,90,135,180,225,270,315} {\draw (3.6,0)--($(3.6,0)+(\a:1.2)$);}
\node at (3.6,-1.7) {Spinner B};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Spinner A has $4$ equal sectors and Spinner B has $8$ equal sectors. Each spinner has exactly one blue sector (shaded). Jackson says, 'The chance of spinning blue is the same on each spinner, because each spinner has one blue sector.' Explain why Jackson is incorrect.
